  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Tue Jun 9 2026\n\nAn elongated ridge running from Arizona to the central Gulf coast\nwill keep our temperatures above average through the end of the \nweek. High temperatures in the lowlands Wednesday and Thursday \nwill run 5 to 7 degrees above average. This is warm enough to \nwarrant a heat advisory for much of El Paso county and the Rio \nGrande Valley of Hudspeth county. The position of the upper level \nridge will make all the difference on our temperatures for Friday \nthrough Sunday. Right now it looks like the upper level ridge will\nretreat to the east a bit and this will allow some moisture to \nslip back into the region. High temperature for this weekend will \ndrop back closer to normal with a chance for rain both afternoons.\nBut if the ridge strengthens and remains in place, we will stay \ndry and our triple digit heat will continue. For now I will lean \nmore toward the \"cooler\" solution, but we may need to adjust \nthings if the ridge looks like it is anchored here a little \nlonger.\n\nFor the start of next week, an upper level trough moving through \nthe Midwest will squeeze the upper level ridge to our west and \nthat will keep us from getting quite so hot. But the extended \nmodels are indicating that the upper level ridge will build right \nback into the region for the middle and end of next week. So if we\ndo get a break from the 100 degree temperature it looks like it \nwill only be for a few days.",
